      body: "Every block in a blockchain contains two main parts: the block header and the transaction data.\n\nThe block header contains:\n\n• Version — the protocol version, indicating which set of block validation rules to follow.\n\n• Previous block hash — the SHA-256 hash of the previous block's header. This is what creates the 'chain' — each block points back to the one before it. Altering any historical block would change its hash, breaking the chain from that point forward.\n\n• Merkle root — the root hash of the Merkle tree of all transactions in this block. Serves as a compact fingerprint of every transaction.\n\n• Timestamp — when the miner started hashing the block header. Used for difficulty adjustment and ordering.\n\n• Difficulty target — a compact representation of the target hash that miners must beat. Adjusted every 2,016 blocks in Bitcoin (~2 weeks) to maintain an average 10-minute block time.\n\n• Nonce — a 32-bit number that miners increment to try to produce a valid hash below the difficulty target. This is the variable in the proof-of-work puzzle.\n\nThe transaction data contains all the transactions included in the block. The first transaction is always the coinbase transaction — a special transaction that creates new bitcoin and sends the block reward plus accumulated transaction fees to the miner's address.\n\nBitcoin's block size is limited to approximately 1MB (with SegWit effectively allowing up to ~4MB of block weight), which constrains the number of transactions per block to roughly 2,000-3,000."
