      body: "Gas is Ethereum's unit for measuring computational work. Every operation in the EVM has a fixed gas cost: a simple transfer costs 21,000 gas; storing a 256-bit word costs 20,000 gas; addition costs 3 gas. The total gas used multiplied by the gas price (in gwei, a tiny fraction of ETH) determines the transaction fee.\n\nSince EIP-1559 (August 2021), Ethereum uses a dual fee structure: a base fee (set algorithmically based on network demand and burned — permanently destroyed) and a priority fee or 'tip' (paid to validators to incentivise inclusion). The base fee adjusts dynamically — when blocks are more than 50% full, the base fee increases; when less than 50% full, it decreases.\n\nWhy gas matters economically: it creates a market for block space. During high demand (an NFT mint, a DeFi exploit, a market crash), gas prices spike because users compete for limited block space. This means identical transactions cost vastly different amounts depending on network congestion.\n\nGas also prevents spam and infinite loops. Every operation costs real money, so it's economically irrational to flood the network with junk transactions or deploy contracts with infinite loops (they'd simply run out of gas and revert).\n\nThe gas model has significant UX implications. Users must hold ETH to interact with any Ethereum-based application, even if the application uses a different token. This is a major barrier to mainstream adoption and a key reason why Layer 2 solutions (which dramatically reduce gas costs) and account abstraction (which allows gas payment in any token) are critical developments."
