      body: "Smart contract security is uniquely high-stakes because deployed code is immutable, transactions are irreversible, and contracts often hold millions in assets. The major vulnerability classes:\n\nReentrancy — the vulnerability behind The DAO hack. When a contract sends ETH to an external address, the receiving contract can 'call back' into the sending contract before the first execution completes. If the sending contract updates its balance after sending (rather than before), the attacker can recursively drain funds. The fix: update state before making external calls (checks-effects-interactions pattern).\n\nInteger overflow/underflow — in older Solidity versions (pre-0.8.0), arithmetic operations could silently wrap around. A uint8 variable at 255 plus 1 would become 0, not 256. An attacker could manipulate token balances by triggering overflows. Solidity 0.8.0+ includes built-in overflow checks.\n\nFront-running — because pending transactions are visible in the mempool before being mined, attackers can see a profitable transaction, submit their own with a higher gas price to get executed first, and profit at the original user's expense. This is a subset of MEV (Maximal Extractable Value) — the value that can be extracted by reordering, inserting, or censoring transactions within a block.\n\nAccess control failures — functions that should be restricted to administrators being accidentally left public. Parity Wallet's $30M loss in 2017 resulted from an unprotected initialisation function that anyone could call.\n\nOracle manipulation — feeding bad price data to exploit DeFi protocols. Flash loan attacks often use this: borrow a massive amount, manipulate a price oracle, exploit a protocol that relies on that oracle, repay the loan — all in a single transaction.\n\nBest practices include: professional audits before deployment, formal verification for high-value contracts, bug bounty programs, time-locked upgrades, and multi-sig admin controls."
