      heading: "Layer 1 vs Layer 2 scaling",
      body: "The blockchain trilemma states that you can optimise for two of three properties: scalability, security, and decentralisation. Layer 2 solutions attempt to break this trade-off by building on top of a secure Layer 1 (like Ethereum) while dramatically increasing throughput.\n\nOptimistic rollups (Arbitrum, Optimism, Base) execute transactions off-chain but post transaction data to Ethereum. They 'optimistically' assume transactions are valid. If someone believes a transaction is fraudulent, they can submit a fraud proof within a challenge period (typically 7 days). The economic security: challengers who prove fraud earn a reward; fraudulent sequencers lose their bond. Trade-off: the 7-day withdrawal delay for moving assets back to Layer 1.\n\nZK-rollups (zkSync, StarkNet, Polygon zkEVM) also execute off-chain but use zero-knowledge proofs to mathematically prove that all transactions are valid. No challenge period needed — the proof itself is the guarantee. Trade-off: generating ZK proofs is computationally intensive (though this cost is amortised across thousands of transactions), and ZK technology is more complex to build for general-purpose computation.\n\nSidechains (Polygon PoS) are independent blockchains with their own consensus mechanisms that connect to the main chain via a bridge. They offer high throughput and low fees but have weaker security guarantees because they don't inherit the main chain's security.\n\nState channels (Lightning Network for Bitcoin, Raiden for Ethereum) allow two parties to transact off-chain by locking funds in a smart contract, conducting unlimited transactions between themselves, and then settling the final state on-chain. Extremely fast and cheap for repeated transactions between the same parties. Trade-off: requires both parties to be online and only works for direct bilateral relationships.\n\nThe scaling landscape is evolving rapidly. As of 2025-26, Ethereum's rollup-centric roadmap is the dominant approach, with Base (Coinbase's L2) and Arbitrum leading in TVL and transaction volume."
