      body: "Decentralised Finance (DeFi) is the collective term for financial services built on public blockchains that operate without traditional intermediaries — no banks, no brokers, no clearinghouses. Instead, smart contracts execute financial logic automatically.\n\nThe power of DeFi lies in composability — often called 'money legos.' Because every DeFi protocol is an open smart contract on a public blockchain, any protocol can interact with any other protocol without permission. A user can borrow DAI from Aave, swap it for ETH on Uniswap, stake that ETH in Lido for stETH, and use stETH as collateral on Compound — all in a single transaction. This permissionless interoperability is impossible in traditional finance, where every integration requires bilateral agreements, APIs, and legal contracts.\n\nThe DeFi stack from bottom to top:\n\n1. Settlement layer — the base blockchain (Ethereum, Solana, etc.)\n2. Asset layer — the tokens (ETH, ERC-20 tokens, stablecoins)\n3. Protocol layer — the smart contracts implementing financial logic (Uniswap, Aave, Maker)\n4. Application layer — the front-end interfaces users interact with\n5. Aggregation layer — platforms that combine multiple protocols for optimal execution (1inch, Yearn)\n\nTotal Value Locked (TVL) is the primary metric for measuring DeFi adoption — it represents the total value of assets deposited in DeFi protocols. As of early 2026, DeFi TVL across all chains exceeds $150 billion, with Ethereum-based protocols holding the majority."
