      body: "Flash loans are the most alien concept in DeFi — they have no analogy in traditional finance. A flash loan lets you borrow any amount of any asset with zero collateral, as long as you repay it within the same blockchain transaction.\n\nHow is this possible? Because of atomic transactions. In blockchain, a transaction either executes completely or reverts entirely. If the flash loan isn't repaid by the end of the transaction, the entire transaction (including the borrow) is reverted as if it never happened. The protocol faces zero risk of non-repayment.\n\nLegitimate uses include: arbitrage (borrow millions, exploit a price difference between two exchanges, repay the loan, pocket the difference — all in one transaction), collateral swaps (swap the collateral backing a DeFi loan without closing and reopening the position), and self-liquidation (repay your own at-risk loan more cheaply than waiting for a liquidator).\n\nAttack vectors: flash loans have enabled some of the largest DeFi exploits. The pattern: borrow a huge amount via flash loan, use it to manipulate a price oracle (by making a massive trade that moves the price), exploit a protocol that relies on that oracle, profit, repay the loan. Notable examples include bZx ($8M, 2020), Cream Finance ($130M, 2021), and numerous others. The total value extracted through flash loan attacks exceeds $1 billion.\n\nFlash loans highlight a fundamental tension in DeFi: the same composability that makes the ecosystem powerful also creates systemic risks. When every protocol is interconnected, a vulnerability in one can cascade across many."
