      body: "Stablecoins are cryptocurrencies designed to maintain a stable value, typically pegged to a fiat currency like the US dollar. They solve a fundamental problem: you can't build financial services on a currency that fluctuates 10% in a day. As of 2026, the total stablecoin market cap exceeds $200 billion, with USDT and USDC dominating.\n\nFiat-backed stablecoins (USDT by Tether, USDC by Circle) are the simplest model. For every token in circulation, the issuer holds an equivalent amount of fiat currency (or short-term liquid assets like US Treasury bills) in reserve. The peg is maintained by arbitrage: if USDC trades above $1, arbitrageurs mint new USDC by depositing dollars, increasing supply until the price returns to $1. If it trades below $1, they redeem USDC for dollars, reducing supply. USDC is widely considered more transparent — Circle publishes monthly reserve attestations and is regulated as a money transmitter. USDT has faced persistent questions about reserve quality, though it remains the most traded stablecoin by volume.\n\nCrypto-collateralised stablecoins (DAI by MakerDAO) are backed by cryptocurrency locked in smart contracts rather than fiat in bank accounts. Because crypto is volatile, these stablecoins are overcollateralised — to mint 100 DAI ($100), you might lock $150 worth of ETH. If the collateral value drops, the position is automatically liquidated. DAI is more decentralised than USDC (no company can freeze your DAI) but more capital-inefficient.\n\nAlgorithmic stablecoins attempt to maintain their peg through supply-and-demand mechanisms without any collateral. The most infamous was TerraUST, which used a mint-and-burn mechanism with LUNA: when UST fell below $1, arbitrageurs could burn UST for $1 worth of LUNA, reducing UST supply. This worked until a massive sell-off overwhelmed the mechanism, creating a 'death spiral' where both UST and LUNA crashed to near zero — destroying $40 billion in value in May 2022. The Terra collapse effectively discredited the uncollateralised algorithmic model."
