      heading: "PSD3 and blockchain convergence",
      body: "The European Commission's Payment Services Directive 3 (PSD3) and the accompanying Payment Services Regulation (PSR) represent the next evolution of open banking — and they create both opportunities and requirements for blockchain payment providers.\n\nPSD3 key changes relevant to blockchain: expanded scope that may cover crypto-asset transfers (depending on final text), stronger customer authentication requirements, enhanced API performance standards for account access, and new rules for payment initiation services. The regulation explicitly acknowledges that payment systems are evolving beyond traditional models.\n\nOpen banking APIs allow authorised third parties to access bank account data (with customer consent) and initiate payments directly. This is relevant to blockchain because:\n\nOn-ramp/off-ramp integration — blockchain payment providers can use open banking APIs to connect fiat bank accounts with blockchain wallets, enabling seamless movement between traditional and crypto rails.\n\nAccount-to-account (A2A) payments on-chain — instead of card payments (which incur 1.5-3.5% merchant fees), A2A payments initiated through open banking APIs and settled on blockchain rails could reduce costs to near zero. This is the model companies like Modulr are exploring.\n\nRequest-to-pay (RtP) with smart contracts — a payee sends a payment request that includes smart contract logic (conditional release, escrow, split payments). The payer authorises via their bank's open banking API, and the blockchain handles the conditional execution.\n\nVariable Recurring Payments (VRPs) are particularly interesting for blockchain integration. VRPs allow merchants to pull variable amounts from a customer's account (unlike direct debits which are fixed). Combined with smart contract logic, VRPs could enable usage-based billing, dynamic subscription pricing, and automated portfolio rebalancing — all with the customer maintaining control through their bank's consent dashboard."
