      heading: "Enterprise treasury and ISO 20022",
      body: "Enterprise treasury management — managing a company's cash positions, FX exposures, investments, and liquidity across multiple accounts, currencies, and geographies — is ripe for blockchain-based automation.\n\nSmart contract-driven cash pooling: multinational corporations maintain cash across dozens of bank accounts in different currencies. Physical cash pooling (moving money between accounts to optimise balances) requires daily sweeps, bank coordination, and reconciliation. Smart contracts could automatically balance positions across accounts based on predefined rules — minimum balances, FX thresholds, investment parameters — executing continuously rather than in daily batches.\n\nAutomated FX hedging: a smart contract could monitor FX exposures in real-time and automatically execute hedging transactions when thresholds are breached. Combined with DeFi FX protocols, this could reduce hedging costs and latency.\n\nLiquidity management: tokenised money market instruments (like BlackRock's BUIDL) allow treasurers to move between cash and yield-bearing positions instantly, 24/7. Traditional money market funds require T+1 settlement and cut-off times. Tokenised alternatives settle in seconds.\n\nISO 20022 is the emerging global standard for financial messaging, replacing legacy formats like MT (SWIFT) and BAI. Its relevance to blockchain: ISO 20022 messages carry rich, structured data (detailed remittance information, purpose codes, regulatory data). Blockchain transactions historically carry minimal metadata. The convergence: blockchain payment rails that generate ISO 20022-compliant messages, enabling interoperability between on-chain and traditional systems. SWIFT's migration to ISO 20022 (completed November 2025 for cross-border payments) creates a common data language that bridges blockchain and traditional infrastructure.\n\nIndia's UPI (Unified Payments Interface) is relevant context: UPI processes over 10 billion transactions monthly with near-zero cost, demonstrating that real-time, low-cost payments are possible without blockchain. The question for blockchain in India is whether it adds value beyond what UPI already provides — the answer lies in cross-border payments, programmability, and interoperability with global DeFi rails rather than competing with domestic UPI."
